In article <2cg4g4$rjs@s.ms.uky.edu>, Wes Morgan <morgan@engr.uky.edu> wrote:
>I used this experience (I'm a veteran) in the design of this idea.  The
>way I see it, I have three choices:
>
>	- Search on suspicion (I immediately discarded this option),
>	- Search everyone on a semi-regular basis, or
>	- Search random groups.
>
>Perhaps it would be better to take a random approach; it may even in-
>crease the efficiency of the process.  What if, instead of searching
>all users' files, we only searched the files of a random 5% of the users
>each time?

Well, since the real intent probably isn't to prevent people from
owning copies of 'prohibited' code (an idea I find extremely
distasteful, since code is 'published material'), but rather to
prevent people from running such code, and since stored files can be
disguised quite easily anyway (even a very simple encryption will foil
your search), how about randomly searching /dev/kmem for signature
strings instead?  That way you can catch people executing 'illegal'
programs, not just owning 'banned' subject-matter.

As to the technical feasibility... well, I just started thinking about
it, but obviously scripts would be easy to catch this way, binary
exectuables would be harder... but then that's true for searching
stored files as well.  Also, the issue of privacy still arises... but
if you searched only 'text' segments (is an executable script 'text'
or 'data'?)  you could probably have a policy that anything that gets
executed by the processor is considered public and subject to
examination.  Note that this does /not/ condone anything like
keystroke monitoring...  keystrokes are input data.  But then again,
on an abstract level the distinction between 'data' and 'program'
often blurs (and in practice it does also with languages like Lisp and
Tcl, or anything else that has an 'eval' construct.)

I really don't like the direction this is all going.  I understand and
sympathize with Wes's desire to prohibit people from running 'Crack'
or similar programs on his CPU, but then again, half the people I know
around here who might do that have their own Linux boxes on which they
can do this just as easily (and faster than on any of our CPUs that
they'd have to share with others.)  So really not all that much can be
accomplished by declaring certain software 'contraband', except that
we introduce an oodle of sensitive privacy issues.
