I would say that a 24-hour notice is not only desirable, but
highly appropriate.  If the point of the audit is to use the
university staff's time to "catch" duplicators of illegal software
on behalf of software publishers, then by all means use the 
"gotcha" approach.  However, if the point is to ensure that the
university (or other employer) has some system in effect to minimize
the installation of unlicensed commercial software (or use of 
shareware beyond a reasonable trial period) on its computers, then
there should be no problem with giving "violators" time to erase
the "evidence".

I work for a Federal government agency, and when we had an audit
last year, we probably had several weeks' notice that an audit would
be performed.  First we were told that it would take place, but not
where or when.  Then we were told approximately when, but not which
agencies, or which locations within agencies would be subject to 
inspection.  Then we were told which agencies, and which locations,
and given an approximate date (week of...), and were also informed
that not all machines would be inspected.  Finally, we were given
the exact date and time and location, and a list of the serial numbers
of the machines to be inspected.

By that time, anyone who had not removed unlicensed software probably
didn't know that it was there, or did not know that it was illegal.

Which raises a number of questions.  So you find a machine
that has unlicensed software installed on it.  Is it an "extra" 
copy of a package for which you have a limited site license?  Or is
it something that the organization has never owned?  Is there 
exclusive access to the machine?  Are you going to try to penalize
the user of the machine?  How?  What rights do they have?  What is
the process, and what are the consequences to the organization if
it takes disciplinary action and is later shown to have made 
a mistake (sometimes only on the basis of a procedural error)?

Clearly stating the organization's opposition to use of unlicensed 
and/or unapproved software on its machines, establishing a procedure 
to identify and remove such software from those machines, and then 
following through on that procedure consistently and reliably is the 
key to dealing with this problem, not trying to "catch" people 
installing and/or using illegal software on those machines.
