In article <2ck76g$cbo@fido.asd.sgi.com>, nelson@bolyard.wpd.sgi.com (Nelson
Bolyard) says [concerning Wes Morgan's sample CERT-replacement banner #2]:
>
>
>My reading of the above banner is that "sometimes I will have privacy,
>sometimes I won't, and I won't generally be able to tell."   And in my mind,
>after reading the above, I have _no_ expectation of privacy.
>
>But it seems to me that the "expectation of privacy" is a binary thing.
>At any point in time, I either have it or I don't.  It's like being pregnant.
>Having privacy most of the time, except once-in-a-while, is like not being
>pregnant most of the time, except once-in-a-while.  If I cannot tell when I
>have privacy and when I don't, then (being paranoid ;-) I never expect to
>have privacy.  Some of our net.lawyers may wish to comment on the binary
>nature (or lack thereof) of the "expectation of privacy".

     I'm a net.philosopher rather than a net.lawyer, but feel
compelled to get my spare change in here.

     Expectations can be relatively weak or strong, whether they
be expectations of privacy or of a horse as a birthday gift.  If
your position is: "If there is any reason not to expect complete
privacy protection then you have no expectation of privacy," then
perhaps *you* typically have no expectation of privacy.  What the
law (and life?) worry about is the *reasonable* expectation of privacy.
It is unclear to me whether this should be treated as binary, i.e.,
whether part of the function of "reasonable" here is to slip cases
into either the A or B bins depending on the degree of expectation
of privacy.

     Where I do strongly agree with you is that if the banner does
not unambiguously tell users in what circumstances their activites
will be monitored, it is not doing its job properly.

     So, I like your suggestion:
>
>To my way of thinking, you need two banners, one that reads (in short):
>
>| We're not monitoring your session, this time, but the next time you login
>| we might.  If we ever do, we'll let you know, when you login, that we're
>| doing it so that you can decided to continue, or not, at that time.
>
>And another banner that says:
>
>| We're monitoring login sessions at this time, and anything you do while
>| logged in to _this_ session will be monitored to some degree.
>| If you don't want to be monitored, logoff and coe back later.
>
>I find both of those banners to be unambiguous.  One sets up a clear
>expectation of privacy.  The other says I have none.  It's clear, it's
>short, and it's simple.  It doesn't hedge, hem-and-haw.
>

     The rub, of course, is that a banner that says, "We think
there may be a criminal using the system right now" warns the
criminal using the system right now, if any.  That made perfect
sense to me, but I have to admit that your gloss strikes me as
awfully similar, and it doesn't make me as happy.

[Your gloss:]
>
>Now, if the counterargument is that ambiguous banners deter cybercrime,
>I would remind the reader that the purpose of the CERT banner was not
>to deter cybercrime, but rather to absolve the (governmental) sysops
>from liability for monitoring when their users had an expectation of
>privacy.  Their purpose was solely to eliminate that expectation.
>
>So, whaddaya think?
>--
     Well, now I'm confused, frankly.

