Hello, I'm a student at the University of Iowa. First of all, if
this isn't the appropriate newsgroup for this, could you point me
in the right direction? Thanks. I'll present a brief overview first,
then follow up with more detailed info following the dotted line.

Recently, the Iowa Board of Regents, the committee which oversees
Iowa's three public universities, enacted a policy in which students
would have to be warned in advance of sexually-oriented class
material which could possibly offend them--not what is generally
accepted as pornography necessarily, but any material dealing with
sex. This could be interpreted as including medical texts describing
the transmission of AIDS, slides showing syphilis lesions, and
psychology textbooks on Freudian theory.

Under the policy, students would not only be warned of the
material, but they would also have the right to walk out of
class should they be offended.

The Iowa Board of Regents was recently challenged to a
public debate concerning the policy. The Board declined,
claiming that the policy was not a public matter.

If you have ideas on how to combat this repressive (IMHO) policy,
please contact me by e-mail at mlara@umaxc.weeg.uiowa.edu since
I rarely go to this newsgroup. Thanks.

. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 

This policy has come about from three incidents within the past two
years, in which a student complained about class material.

In one case, a student complained after watching the film "Taxi
zum Klo" in German class, a film which apparently was shown in
German high schools. Instead of protesting directly to the
instructor, as mandated by University policy, she complained
to her mother, who subsequently sent a letter of complaint to
the governor and to the local media. The matter came to the
attention of the Iowa Board of Regents, who demanded an
investigation. The incident ended with a reprimand of the
teaching assistant.

Another incident occurred in an art class, where a visiting
artist presented a film which included scenes of homosexual
intercourse.

This chain of events was repeated again in the spring of this
year, when an American Studies TA was reprimanded for showing
"Paris Is Burning", a film which presents transvestite life,
but shows NO explicit sex whatsoever.

After these incidents, much noise was made by the
school administration about the desire to embrace diversity,
while at the same time respecting the sensitivities of
students. Later this year, no complaint at all was made
by the administration when a local right-wing student group
advertised a T-shirt calling on people to stop AIDS by
killing homosexuals. 

The administration of this school and the Iowa Board of
Regents have established a pattern of selective vision
with regards to the terms "academic freedom" and "student
rights". This pattern, as of last month, is now written
down in policy. It is my belief that this policy is a
thinly-veiled attempt to suppress free discourse in
the name of student rights. Furthermore, I believe that
this policy is an example of the insidious politicization
of this classroom on the part of certain right-wing
elements.

If you believe that this new policy constitutes
censorship, could you please contact me via e-mail as to
its possible social and legal ramifications? I would
also appreciate opinions on how to fight this policy.
I don't go to this newsgroup very often, so contact me
solely by e-mail. Thank you for your attention.

"That's one strike. This is the second strike. I'm going
to tell you, the Board of Regents one of these days is 
going to give you three strikes and out. Somebody is
going to get fired around this university if they don't
follow the rules."
	--Marvin Pomerantz, President, Board of Regents
