First I would like to say, that I think the article was too long
proportional to the content!

Of course the best solutions to the problems mentioned are
legislative. From a Danish viewpoint (everybody probably knows that
Denmark is quite liberal concerning pornography) the statement "that
the depiction of sex for sex sake, is by its very nature, debasing,
disgusting and obscene" (quote from Court of Appeal, the Crown in the
original article) seems rediculous. BUT even from a Danish point of view
there exists a problem with the Internet, and that is the possibility of
abusing anonymity.

From a legal point of view computer networks should not in any way be
responsible for what individuals distribute on them, the individuals
should. Just like the telephone company cannot be responsible for
what you say in the phone...!
For an Internet user it is obvious that it is not the computers or
system operators, that decide to break the law, but the sender of an
article/e-mail.

_So the whole puzzle comes down to one problem:_

The sender of an article cannot for certain be found and held
responsible for the content of his posting, simply because
header-lines can be forged. One could argue that it is also because of
anonymity-servers, but that is a more advanced discussion, where
pseudonymity-servers should be considered, because it is a very
important feature that your identity can be protected!

The certain (one-to-one) connection between a living person and an
e-mail address would actually be an advantage to Internet users.
Together with safe encryption and authentication of messages, like the
public-key-encryption (implemented in Pretty Good Privacy (PGP)) it
would make it possible to make business contracts, orders etc.
directly through the net.

The implementation of such features on the Internet should have the
highest priority for the Internet community!

(I remember reading that Apple (I think) was implementing
authentication into Mac-networks.)

