In article <hero.754115433@login.dkuug.dk> hero@login.dkuug.dk (Henrik Roseno) writes:
|From a legal point of view computer networks should not in any way be
|responsible for what individuals distribute on them, the individuals
|should. Just like the telephone company cannot be responsible for
|what you say in the phone...!
|For an Internet user it is obvious that it is not the computers or
|system operators, that decide to break the law, but the sender of an
|article/e-mail.
|
|_So the whole puzzle comes down to one problem:_
|
|The sender of an article cannot for certain be found and held
|responsible for the content of his posting, simply because
|header-lines can be forged. One could argue that it is also because of
|anonymity-servers, but that is a more advanced discussion, where
|pseudonymity-servers should be considered, because it is a very
|important feature that your identity can be protected!

But, just like with the telephone company, there is no absolute
prerequisite for being able to identify the originator of a
transmission.

Until recently, phone calls could not always be traced.

They could if they were repeated often enough to get a court
order to put a trace on the line and then were repeated after
that time.

A single call was essentially anonymous unless the caller was
willing to identify themselves and did so honestly, or if the
callee was able to recognize the caller from their voice or
some other part of what they heard during the call.

Now, after decades of living with essential technology that
had not been able to prevent some degree of anonymity, the
phone companies have the ability (for a decade or more in many
places) of tracing calls after they occurred (Call Trace), or
even before they occur (Caller ID).  (And there is a great
deal of resentment from some people about losing that anonymity
- after all, who wants to be put on a hundred mailing lists
just because they called a store for information.)

Thus, the fact that messages can be forged and the originator
be difficult or impossible to determine (or especially to
prove for sure that the originator has been *correctly*
determined) does not justify applying the law to the carrier.

I'm not sure that the law should have significant distinction
between spoken illegalities (e.g. those delivered using a
telephone call) and written illegalities (e.g. those delivered
using an Internet message).
