In article <1993Nov24.135039.6607@accesspoint.north.net>,
Timothy Pinos <tpinos@AccessPoint.North.Net> wrote:
>Mathew Englander (mathew@unixg.ubc.ca) wrote:
>: There's an interesting aspect to the ban that no one has discussed. This
>: is, does it apply outside Ontario? The court that ordered the ban had
>: jurisdiction only within Ontario. As far as I know, there is no federal
>: legislation covering the issue. The Ontario judge who issued the ban
>: apparently assumed that he could do so for all of Canada -- why else
>: would he exclude American media from the trial but not out-of-
>: province media? However the position in law is not as certain.
>: I know of no cases, for example, where someone was charged in, say,
>: British Columbia, for contempt of an Ontario court.
>
>My understanding is that the authority for the judge to issue the ban is from
>the Criminal Code, which is a federal statute. Therefore, such an order
>would be automatically recognized all over the country.

What section of the Criminal Code permits the judge to do this? I can't
find any. Section 542(2) contains a limited publication ban, of sorts, but
it certainly doesn't apply to the Homolka trial. To get around the
extraterritoriality problem, I think there would have to be a federal
legislative provision that specifically said, "No one shall publish
information about the proceedings in a criminal trial anywhere in Canada,
when that information is subject to a publication ban issued by the court
conducting the trial" or words to that effect. I don't think there is any
such provision. Is there?

By the way, s. 542(2) says this:
	
	Every one who publishes in any newspaper, or broadcasts, a report
	that any admission or evidence was tendered in evidence at a 
	preliminary inquiry or a a report of the nature of such admission
	or confession so tendered in evidence unless
		(a) the accused has been discharged, or
		(b) if the accused has been committed for trial, the
		trial has ended,
	is guilty of an offence punishable on summary conviction.


