And arguably not even on publically-funded universities, since they
do not "govern" or in any way directly support the act of governing. 
And the restrictions of the Constitution apply to government, but
not necessarily to any and all of its myriad creatures, regardless
of the function they perform (where they perform any at all).

>>their own property.  "seizure" is especially amusing in this context:
>>I can just see going to a judge to get a court order for permission
>>to seize *your*own* disk drive.

>When the university is a public institution, constitutional protections
>apply.

Except for our venerated Congress, bless their black little hearts,
no institution or person may safely disregard *actual* constitutional
protections. There is also little *actual* evidence to demonstrate
that those protections are any more available in public than in private
institutions -- and there is probably just as much evidence against
(cf. PC language).

Basic protections may be *extended* by interpretation in public policy,
but such interpretation may dramatically alter with a simple change of
political leadership. Such public policy is *not* a constitutional
protection: it is a transitory political decision subject to the whim
of the governing faction (including university administrations). Like-
wise, private policy is not a constitutional protection, either: it
is a transitory management decision subject to the whim of the man-
aging faction.

The fact that funding is public does *not* immediately throw an
automatic blanket of "constitutional protection" over the actions
of either students or staff with respect to their use of assets
such public funding has acquired and maintains. Nor may either
students or staff assume they innately acquire *ownership* rights
by virtue of enjoying usage *privileges*. [This distinction may
be obscure to the terminally socialistic.]

Because some property (in this case a computing facility) is
publically owned does not thus render it automatically free and
equally available to everyone on the planet, or free of restrictions
of use by the administrative authority charged with responsibility
for ensuring compliance with public policy to which the asset is
dedicated. [If you do not believe this, try climbing into one of the
campus police cars and driving it down to the grocery store to do
your shopping, or perhaps walking into the local armory with the
expectation that you will be allowed to check out your choice of
automatic weapons (or anything else) for your own personal use,
and with no questions asked.]
