Newsgroups: mcgill.general
Subject: Uproar at meeting about Student Code changes: Searching files


There was another in the series of meetings today
reviewing proposed changes to the Student Code (Green Book).
There is a whole laundry list of changes, and some are very significant.

The group is methodically discussing all the proposed changes,
with the McGill Legal Information Clinic watching out for student interests.

The very orderly meeting was sent into a brief tizzy when
the Dean of Students commented on whether or not computer files are private.
The source of the disturbance was a comment attributed to the Dean of Law
that the University can, at any time, rifle through the contents
of any professor's office filing cabinets.  Two law professors, and several
other participants exclaimed in loud voices that this was not at all
clear in the law.  One law professor said "THIS IS SCARY!".
After the commotion subsided, another law professor chose his words
carefully and said "I respectfully disagree with my Dean".

This is an important issue, since the Senate Computing Committee
has been repeatedly pointing to this "legal opinion" as if
it was well-established.  This forms the basis of their argument that
computer files, including e-mail are searchable at any time,
without prior approval or notification.

I think given today's meeting, the SCC view is on shakier grounds.

